## Terragrunt 1.x (stargazer) CLI cheatsheet

The framework runs Terragrunt 1.x. Many legacy `--terragrunt-*` flags have
been renamed or removed. Use only what is below.

KNOWN-GOOD INVOCATIONS:
- `terragrunt run --all apply --non-interactive`
- `terragrunt run --all output --non-interactive`
- `terragrunt force-unlock <LOCK_ID>`        (positional id only — no `--force`)
- `terragrunt --working-dir <unit-dir> apply` (single-unit form)

PASSING TERRAFORM FLAGS:
Terraform-specific flags (`-json`, `-lock=false`, `-refresh=false`) MUST come
after a literal `--` separator.
  GOOD: `terragrunt run --all output --non-interactive -- -json > {{outputs_path}}`

FLAGS THAT NO LONGER EXIST (do not invent them):
- `--terragrunt-include-external-dependencies`
- `--terragrunt-non-interactive`     (just `--non-interactive`)
- `--terragrunt-parallelism`         (use `TG_PARALLELISM` env var)
- `--force` on `force-unlock`

IF YOU SEE `flag provided but not defined` or `is not a valid flag for ...`,
STOP. Re-read this cheatsheet. Do NOT retry the same command verbatim.

TIMEOUTS: a full `--all apply` for ~10 modules on a cold cache can take
10-25 minutes (RDS alone can be 5-10 min), and local-model latency can stretch
wall time further. Set `timeout_secs=6000` on every shell call that runs
terragrunt. If Goose rejects this value as too large, fall back to
`timeout_secs=3600` and report the rejection in your next message — do not
silently drop to a smaller number.

## DO NOT use Monitor / background tail for `terragrunt apply`

`terragrunt run --all apply` has a definite end (the process exits when the
last unit finishes or fails). Run it **synchronously** as a single shell
call with a generous `timeout_secs`. Do NOT pipe its log into a `tail -F`
under Monitor or under `run_in_background` — by the time your tail starts,
the apply may have already printed its completion lines, and the tail will
hang forever waiting for new lines that never come. Several real runs have
been wedged this way for 30+ minutes after the apply itself finished.

Synchronous form (the only correct form for this recipe):
```
cd <iac_dir>/environments/<env> && \
  TG_PARALLELISM=1 terragrunt run --all apply --non-interactive
```
Wait for it to return. Read the exit code. Done.

## Task

1. Confirm AWS identity:
   ```
   aws sts get-caller-identity
   ```
   Compare the account id and ARN to the values recorded at the top of
   `{{plan_summary_path}}`. If they differ, STOP — do not apply against a
   different account than the one the operator approved.

2. `cd {{iac_dir}}/environments/{{env}}` and run apply:
   ```
   terragrunt run --all apply --non-interactive
   ```
   Terragrunt walks the dependency graph automatically (vpc → secrets / iam /
   ecr / rds-sg / ecs-cluster → alb / rds → ecs-service). Units that were
   "Deferred to apply" in the plan summary will plan + apply here once their
   dependencies are real.

3. If apply fails part-way through, do NOT run `destroy`. Do NOT run `apply`
   a second time blindly. Report the failed units and the verbatim error and
   stop. The operator will decide whether to retry, fix manually, or roll
   back the partial creation.

4. Outputs collection is **not your job**. The framework wrapper
   (`merge_terragrunt_outputs` in `lib/outputs.sh`) iterates units and
   produces a correctly-merged `{{outputs_path}}` AFTER you emit the
   success marker. The legacy `terragrunt run --all output -- -json >
   {{outputs_path}}` invocation is broken (per-unit JSON gets concatenated,
   invalid as a single document) and must NOT be used here. Just don't
   touch the outputs file.

5. **Print the operator-facing summary FIRST**, then the success marker.
   The bash wrapper greps stdout for `INFRA DEPLOYED`. If you stop
   without printing it (e.g. wedged on a Monitor cleanup), the recipe
   is recorded as failed even though the apply succeeded. Real incident:
   a successful greenfield apply was marked failed because the model
   tried to stop a Monitor before emitting the marker and never reached
   it.

   Order of stdout output:

   a. Apply summary line — count of units that succeeded vs. failed,
      taken verbatim from terragrunt's "Run Summary" stdout. Do NOT
      fabricate endpoints — those will be filled in by the framework
      wrapper from real terragrunt output.

   b. Immediately after (a), print the success marker on its own line:
      ```
      INFRA DEPLOYED env={{env}} region={{region}}
      ```

   c. After (b), cleanup is optional — the goose process exits and any
      leftover Monitor / background tail dies with it. If you must stop
      a Monitor, do so AFTER (b), never before. A stuck cleanup tool
      after the marker is harmless; a stuck cleanup tool before the
      marker invalidates the whole recipe.

## Constraints

- **No re-approval prompts.** The bash gate already happened. Do not write
  "Type apply to proceed" or any equivalent. The operator is not connected
  to your stdin.

- **Anti-hallucination.** Do not write to `{{outputs_path}}`. The
  framework wrapper handles it after you exit. Never fabricate ARNs,
  endpoints, or IDs in your stdout either.

- **Account safety.** If AWS identity in step 1 does not match the account
  the plan was approved against, STOP without applying.

- **Production extra care.** For `{{env}} == prod`, if any unit shows destroy
  or replacement of stateful resources (RDS, secrets) at apply time, STOP and
  report.

- **No destroy on failure.** If apply fails partway, never run `destroy` or
  `state rm`. Report and stop.

- Output language: **English only**.

- When done successfully, print exactly: `INFRA DEPLOYED env={{env}} region={{region}}`
  and stop.
